" A sample lawhrc for the lawh whiteboard. It mirrors its author's Neovim:
" jkl; to move instead of hjkl. To use it, copy it to ~/.config/lawh/lawhrc
" (Linux and macOS) or %APPDATA%\y0sif\lawh\config\lawhrc (Windows).
"
" lawh reads this file at start. One : command a line, the : optional; only
" :set and the mapping commands are allowed. Lines starting with a double
" quote are comments. Every key and command: docs/keys.md.

" <Leader> stands for this key in the mappings below (the default is \).
set leader=<Space>

" Move with jkl; instead of hjkl, on the board, in Visual mode, after d or y,
" and inside text blocks. noremap covers those three modes; Insert mode keeps
" its keys. With noremap, h j k l below run the built-in moves, not each other.
noremap j h
noremap k j
noremap l k
noremap ; l

" Jumps to the nearest object follow the same layout: J left, K down, L up,
" and H right (Shift+; types :, which stays the command line).
noremap J H
noremap K J
noremap L K
noremap H L

" ' takes the place ; has in Vim (repeat the last f or t). lawh has no such
" command yet, so for now ' does nothing.
nnoremap ' ;

" U redoes, as Ctrl+R does.
nnoremap U <C-r>

" Typing j then k quickly in Insert mode leaves it, as <Esc> does. After j,
" lawh waits timeoutlen (1000 ms by default) for the k before typing the j.
inoremap jk <Esc>

" Space then s writes the board.
nnoremap <Leader>s :w<CR>
